{"format_version":1,"partition":28527,"records":{"aufwühlender":{"entries":[["aufwühlender",null,null,null]],"senses":[[588250,"aufwühlender","adj","comparative degree of aufwühlend",0],[588251,"aufwühlender","adj","inflection of aufwühlend:",1],[588252,"aufwühlender","adj","strong/mixed nominative masculine singular",2],[588253,"aufwühlender","adj","strong genitive/dative feminine singular",3],[588254,"aufwühlender","adj","strong genitive plural",4]]},"goldbachers":{"entries":[["goldbachers",null,"[ˈɡɔltˌbaxɐs]",null]],"senses":[[705015,"goldbachers","noun","genitive singular of Goldbacher",0],[705016,"goldbachers","name","genitive singular of Goldbacher",1],[705017,"goldbachers","name","plural of Goldbacher",2]],"inflections":[["goldbachers","goldbacher","genitive"]]},"hängematte":{"entries":[["hängematte",null,"/ˈhɛŋəˌmatə/","Alteration of Hamach, Hamaco, which is borrowed, probably via earlier Dutch hamak, from Spanish hamaca, from Taíno *hamaka. See English hammock for more.\nThe Spanish form was reinterpreted as “hang-matt” by popular etymology both in German and in Dutch (see hangmat, also attested as hangmak). The modern German word is thus analysable as a compound of hängen + Matte. Such an adapted form is attested in German earlier than in Dutch, but the origin could be in either language. The formal argument (Dutch hangmat being closer to hamak, hamaca than German Hängematte) should not be overestimated since Hängematte is expectably a secondary adaptation of a dialectal intermediate form (in order to accord with the words and morphology of standard German).",{"g":"f","decl":"||||n|n|n|n"}]],"senses":[[27806,"hängematte","noun","hammock",0,"f"]]},"kegelförmiges":{"entries":[["kegelförmiges",null,null,null]],"senses":[[504678,"kegelförmiges","adj","strong/mixed nominative/accusative neuter singular of kegelförmig",0]],"inflections":[["kegelförmiges","kegelförmig","neuter,nominative,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"strömen durch":{"entries":[["strömen d